// Romanized Hinglish commands (20 cases).
//
// THE GO/NO-GO STRATUM. The architecture document names Hinglish intent
// precision as the project's least-confident bet and puts the Phase 2 decision
// on it at ~85%. These cases are therefore weighted toward realistic phrasing
// rather than toward cases the system is likely to pass.
//
// Two features English does not exercise: verb-final word order (the domain noun
// precedes the imperative, which is why the gate's proximity window is
// bidirectional), and phonetic spelling variation with no authoritative form
// ("banao"/"bnao"/"bana do"/"banado").
//
// TWO CASES BELOW ARE LABELLED TO AN ANSWER THE CURRENT VOCABULARY DOES NOT
// PRODUCE (hin.007, hin.012). That is deliberate. They were found while
// authoring, they are deterministic-layer gaps rather than classifier gaps, and
// M7a measures rather than fixes — so they are labelled to the correct answer
// and are expected to appear as failures in the baseline and in
// golden_failures.md. Labelling them to current behaviour would bake a gap into
// the baseline as though it were correct, which is how an eval stops being able
// to find anything.

